{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Rename `--force-close` to `--force` on `transition` (clean break, no alias)\n\n## Context / Problem\n`rebar claim` spells its gate escape hatch `--force[=<reason>]`. `rebar transition` spells the\nclose-gate escape hatch `--force-close=<reason>` — one concept, two spellings — while ALSO\nalready accepting a separate valueless `--force` that bypasses the start-work (plan-review)\ngate on `open -> in_progress`. So `transition` today has two different force flags with two\ndifferent meanings, and neither matches `claim`. Agents and docs get it wrong.\n\nOperator decision 2026-08-07 (recorded as a comment on this ticket): **clean break, no\ndeprecation alias.** A stale caller must fail loudly with an explicit error, not keep working.\n\n## Approach\nCollapse `transition`'s two force flags into ONE `--force[=<reason>]` whose meaning is\n\"bypass whichever gate this transition would hit\", dispatched by target status:\n\n- `open -> in_progress`: bypasses the start-work / plan-review gate (today's `--force`\n  behaviour, unchanged). The audit note is the `--force=<reason>` value when given, else the\n  `--reason` text, else `(no reason given)` — so existing `--force --reason=X` invocations\n  keep their exact current behaviour.\n- `-> closed`: bypasses the completion-verification / signature gate (today's\n  `--force-close=<reason>`). A bare `--force` supplies `(no reason given)`, matching `claim`.\n\nThis resolves the collision rather than introducing one: on a close, the valueless `--force`\nis currently parsed and then silently discarded (`force` is only read on the start-work path),\nso no existing close invocation changes meaning except by gaining the bypass it was already\nasking for by name.\n\n`transition`'s flag loop is hand-rolled (`_parse_flags`) and **silently skips unknown tokens**,\nso a plain removal would make `--force-close=\"reason\"` a silent no-op — the worst outcome. The\nparser therefore gets an explicit rejecting branch for both `--force-close` and\n`--force-close=<v>` that raises a `CommandError` naming `--force`. Because the loop matches\ntokens exactly (no prefix/abbreviation matching), no abbreviation hazard is introduced; this\nis complementary to, and does not conflict with, ticket 424f's `allow_abbrev=False` work on\nthe argparse-based commands.\n\nThe library kwarg `rebar.transition(..., force_close=...)` and the internal `force_close`\nPython identifiers are **out of scope** and keep their names — this ticket is about the CLI\nflag string. The `_lib_writes` docstring that calls `force_close` \"the library counterpart of\nthe CLI `--force-close`\" is updated to say `--force`.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py` — `_parse_flags`: single `--force[=<reason>]`, explicit\n  rejection of `--force-close`; `_USAGE` text.\n- `src/rebar/_cli/help/transition.txt` — usage line.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py`, `close_precheck.py`, `txn.py`,\n  `close_disposition.py`, `_lib_writes.py`, `_config_schema.py`, `llm/**` — replace the\n  `--force-close` **flag spelling** in operator-facing error text, docstrings and comments.\n- `scripts/canary_bridge.py` — the two argv builders that emit `--force-close=<reason>`.\n- `docs/cli-reference.md` — regenerated via `scripts/gen_cli_reference.py` (CI drift gate).\n- `README.md`, `rebar.toml`, `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, `docs/manifest-signing.md`,\n  `docs/bug-creation-contract.md`, `docs/remediation-implementation-plan.md`,\n  `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md` — flag references.\n- `CHANGELOG.md` — a breaking-change entry.\n- `tests/` — existing tests that assert the old spelling, plus the new tests below.\n\n## Testing\n- Happy path: `--force=\"reason\"` on a close sets the force-close reason and bypasses the\n  completion gate; `--force` on `open -> in_progress` still bypasses the start-work gate.\n- Bare `--force` on a close yields the `(no reason given)` reason (parity with `claim`).\n- Edge/failure: `--force-close=\"x\"` and bare `--force-close` each raise a `CommandError`\n  whose message names `--force`, and are NOT silently skipped by the unknown-token loop.\n- Regression: `--force --reason=\"x\"` on `open -> in_progress` still uses `x` as the audit note.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `rebar transition <id> <from> closed --force=\"<reason>\"` bypasses the close gate exactly\n      as `--force-close=<reason>` did — verified by a unit test asserting `verify_completion`\n      is never invoked on that path.\n- [ ] A bare `--force` on a close bypasses the gate with the reason `(no reason given)`,\n      matching `rebar claim --force` — verified by a `_parse_flags` unit test.\n- [ ] `--force` on `open -> in_progress` still bypasses the start-work gate, and\n      `--force --reason=\"x\"` still records `x` as the audit note — verified by unit test.\n- [ ] `--force-close` (with or without a value) exits non-zero with an error message\n      containing `--force` — verified by a unit test; it must not be silently skipped.\n- [ ] `grep -rn -- \"--force-close\"` over the tracked tree returns no hits other than the\n      `CHANGELOG.md` entry describing the rename and the parser's rejection message/test.\n- [ ] `docs/cli-reference.md` is regenerated so the CI drift gate passes.\n- [ ] `CHANGELOG.md` records the rename under a breaking-change heading.\n- [ ] No force flag is exposed over MCP — verified by grepping the MCP tool schemas."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786097923046697001,"uuid":"15941ccb-bf7f-4fa7-a786-4c83a8dfe74e"}